.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1151 PM PDT Mon Sep 7 2026
(Tonight through Wednesday)

24 hour trends are warmer and drier. Already a warming trend is
underway with increasing thermal and height ridging aloft. Any
marine layer influence is confined to within a few hundred feet
of sea level with only a few patches of fog along the Big Sur
coast at the moment.

The large scale pattern shift to warmer/hotter temperatures
involves both the retrogression (westward motion) of the
subtropical ridge over the central U.S. and the eventual merging
of tropical energy and moisture from steadily decaying TS Marie
and a cut-off low far to our west (also forecast to retrogress).
Strengthening 500 mb heights, large scale compression and the Sep
sun angle will raise temperatures up further Tuesday, peaking well
above normal both Wednesday and Thursday. A little bit of a
wildcard to this week`s pattern is the northward intrusion of
tropical moisture including some warm air advection (far from
decaying TS Marie) which will be west of the 500 mb ridge axis.
Higher to much higher precipitable water will arrive here, model
forecasts are still showing near 1.40" in the Bay Area mid-week,
near the max moving average for Oakland upper air early-mid Sep
climatology. Mid and high clouds will increase, but we don`t
really know quite yet with high confidence how well the models may
or may not be handling mid-level convective potential (mucape)
with disagreement among various output from e.g. from the HRRR and
GFS. Good to see the GFS shows very minimal to no mucape anyhow.
Also, with hotter surface temperatures (greater internal energy)
air parcels will stay capped as long as the atmosphere is stable.

Hot temperatures for this week are not quite in reach of long term
record highs at this point, except Half Moon Bay may get close if
there`s no shallow sea breeze mid-week as an offset to the
temperature. Will continue to monitor.

A Heat Advisory is in effect for the entire forecast area from 10
AM Wednesday to 10 PM Thursday. We`ve had a nice 10 to 11 day run
of below normal temperatures (due to a large scale trough),
especially inland, so the sudden reversal in temperatures will be
noticeable if not already. With widespread moderate to major
HeatRisk, this affects most individuals sensitive to heat,
especially those without effective cooling and/or adequate
hydration. Please stay out of direct sunlight, and stay properly
hydrated. Hot conditions can cause heat illnesses.

Also, minor coastal flooding is likely within a small window of
time of about 1 hour nearest high tide Tuesday (possibly Wednesday
as well) for bayshore locations of Marin County. Impacts will
include minor flooding of the lowest lying areas directly along
the shoreline. At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is
expected to be 1 ft above normal at 9:59 PM 9/8. At this time we
are messaging it here and will include messaging in social media
vs issuing a coastal flood advisory for Tuesday.

The Beach Hazards Statement was allowed to expire.

